.TH V23-RPC 2
.SH NAME
V23rpc: Client, Request, serve, serveconn, dial, call, close \- Vanadium-style RPC with blessing handshake
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "v23.m";
v23sec := load V23security V23security->PATH;
v23rpc := load V23rpc V23rpc->PATH;

Request: adt {
    method: string;
    arg:    string;
    remote: list of string;
    reply:  chan of (string, string);  # (result, error)
};

Client: adt {
    principal:  ref V23security->Principal;
    fd:         ref Sys->FD;
    remote:     list of string;
};

init:       fn(): string;
serve:      fn(addr: string, p: ref V23security->Principal,
               allow: list of string, reqc: chan of ref Request): string;
serveconn:  fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, p: ref V23security->Principal,
               allow: list of string, reqc: chan of ref Request): string;
dial:       fn(addr: string, p: ref V23security->Principal):
               (ref Client, string);
call:       fn(c: ref Client, method: string, arg: string):
               (string, string);
close:      fn(c: ref Client);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B V23rpc
provides a minimal RPC layer: mutual blessing exchange, pattern ACL, then
request/response calls carrying a single string argument and result.
.B Init
loads
.IR dial (2),
.IR msgio (2),
and
.IR v23-security (2).
.SS Server
.B Serve
announces on
.I addr
(e.g.
.BR tcp!*!3242 ),
accepts connections, and for each connection runs
.B serveconn
in a new process.
.I Allow
is a list of blessing patterns; callers must present a recognizable blessing
matching at least one pattern
.RB ( authorize
in
.IR v23-security (2)).
.PP
If
.I allow
contains
.B ...
.RB ( AllPrincipals ),
the server trusts presented client blessing roots (TOFU) before verification.
This is convenient for demos and unsafe for production.
.PP
Application code receives
.B Request
values on
.IR reqc .
For each request, send
.BI ( result , nil )
or
.BI ( nil , error )
on
.IB r .reply .
.B Serveconn
handles a single already-accepted
.IR fd .
.SS Client
.B Dial
connects to
.IR addr ,
sends the principal’s default blessings, receives the server’s, and verifies
them against the principal’s roots.
On success it returns a
.B Client
whose
.B remote
lists recognized server blessing names.
.PP
.B Call
sends
.I method
and
.I arg
and returns
.BI ( result , nil )
or
.BI ( nil , error ) .
.B Close
sends
.B CLOSE
and drops the descriptor.
.PP
Clients that do not already trust the server root must add it (e.g. via
.B addroot
after an out-of-band exchange, or a TOFU helper as in
.IR v23-fortune (1)).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/v23/rpc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR v23-intro (2),
.IR v23-security (2),
.IR v23 (6),
.IR v23-fortuned (8),
.IR dial (2),
.IR msgio (2)
.SH BUGS
.IP \(bu
Only one string argument and one string result; no streaming, timeouts, or
retries.
.IP \(bu
No channel encryption; anyone on the path can read method/args after the
blessing exchange.
.IP \(bu
.B Dial
does not TOFU server roots; unrecognized servers fail unless roots were
configured.
.IP \(bu
.B Serve
never returns after a successful announce; errors after accept are only logged.
.IP \(bu
Handshake and calls share
.IR msgio (2)
string framing with no version negotiation beyond blessing magic
.BR V23BLESS1 .
.IP \(bu
Concurrent
.B call
s on one
.B Client
are not supported.
